  <!-- Section 1 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    1. The Three Tiers of US EV Charging: Speeds, Hardware & True Costs
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    To understand what you will pay to charge an electric car, you must distinguish between the three levels of charging equipment available in the United States. Where and how you plug in determines whether your cost per mile is 4 cents or 14 cents.
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
    <!-- Level 1 -->
    <div class="p-5 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
        <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-md text-[10px] font-black uppercase bg-gray-100 text-gray-800">Level 1 (120V)</span>
        <span class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-500">Standard Outlet</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-2">Household 3-Prong Plug</h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mb-3 leading-relaxed">
        Plugs into any standard 120-volt household wall socket. Delivers 1.2 to 1.9 kW of power, adding roughly <strong>3 to 5 miles of range per hour</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="pt-3 border-t border-gray-100 text-xs font-semibold text-gray-700">
        <div>Charging Cost: <span class="text-emerald-700 font-bold">$0.16 / kWh</span></div>
        <div class="text-[11px] text-gray-500 mt-0.5">Best for: Plug-in hybrids (PHEVs) or emergency top-ups.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Level 2 -->
    <div class="p-5 bg-emerald-50/70 border-2 border-emerald-300 rounded-2xl shadow-sm relative">
      <div class="absolute -top-2.5 right-4 bg-emerald-600 text-white text-[9px] font-black uppercase tracking-wider px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full">
        Most Recommended
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
        <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-md text-[10px] font-black uppercase bg-emerald-200 text-emerald-900">Level 2 (240V)</span>
        <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-800">Overnight Home</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-2">Dedicated 240V Circuit</h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 mb-3 leading-relaxed">
        Operates on 240-volt circuits (similar to an electric clothes dryer) at 32 to 48 Amps. Delivers 7.2 to 11.5 kW, adding <strong>25 to 45 miles of range per hour</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="pt-3 border-t border-emerald-200/60 text-xs font-semibold text-gray-800">
        <div>Charging Cost: <span class="text-emerald-700 font-bold">$0.08 to $0.18 / kWh</span></div>
        <div class="text-[11px] text-emerald-800 mt-0.5">Full overnight refill: ~$5.00 to $11.00. Over 80% of all US charging.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Level 3 DC Fast -->
