  <!-- Section 4 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    4. The Real-World Engineering Factors: Charging Losses & Winter Range
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    When modeling your household budget, experienced EV drivers know that raw battery capacity does not tell the entire story. Three real-world engineering realities influence your electric utility bill:
  </p>

  <ul class="space-y-4 my-6 list-none pl-0">
    <li class="p-4 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl flex items-start gap-3">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-xl bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 flex items-center justify-center font-black text-sm shrink-0">1</span>
      <div>
        <strong class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950 block">Charging Efficiency Loss (~10% Heat & Inversion):</strong>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mt-1 leading-relaxed">
          When alternating current (AC) electricity travels from your home panel through your onboard vehicle charger and is converted to direct current (DC) in the battery, approximately <strong>8% to 12% of electrical energy is dissipated as heat</strong>. If your Tesla Model Y draws 75 kWh to charge from 0% to 100%, your utility meter will actually register approximately 82.5 kWh. Our calculations include this real-world factor.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="p-4 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl flex items-start gap-3">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-xl bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 flex items-center justify-center font-black text-sm shrink-0">2</span>
      <div>
        <strong class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950 block">Sub-Zero Winter Temperatures (15% to 25% Reduction):</strong>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mt-1 leading-relaxed">
          In cold northern states (like Minnesota, Michigan, and New York), chemical ion resistance increases and resistive cabin heat pumps consume energy to warm the interior. Winter efficiency can drop from 3.8 mi/kWh to 2.9 mi/kWh. However, because gas engines also suffer a 12% to 20% winter mileage drop due to winter-blend fuel and cold oil drag, EVs still maintain a decisive 50%+ cost advantage.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="p-4 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl flex items-start gap-3">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-xl bg-purple-100 text-purple-700 flex items-center justify-center font-black text-sm shrink-0">3</span>
      <div>
        <strong class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950 block">Phantom Drain & Active Security:</strong>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mt-1 leading-relaxed">
          Features like Tesla Sentry Mode (which monitors 8 optical cameras for vandalism) and Cabin Overheat Protection consume roughly 1.5 to 2.5 kWh per 24-hour cycle. At standard rates, this equates to roughly 25¢ to 40¢ per day in standby security monitoring.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Section 5 -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    5. The $7,500 Federal Tax Credit: Instant Point-of-Sale Discount
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Under Section 30D of the Internal Revenue Code, enacted by the Inflation Reduction Act, eligible new clean vehicles qualify for up to a <strong>$7,500 federal clean vehicle tax credit</strong>.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    <strong>Instant Point-of-Sale Transfer:</strong> In prior years, EV buyers had to wait until filing their annual IRS tax return to receive the credit. Today, buyers can transfer the full credit directly to registered dealerships at the moment of purchase, effectively lowering the vehicle purchase price or loan down payment by $7,500 immediately.
  </p>

  <div class="p-5 bg-gradient-to-r from-emerald-50 via-teal-50/50 to-white border-2 border-emerald-200 rounded-2xl not-prose my-6">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-black text-emerald-950 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Federal EV Tax Credit Rules Summary</h3>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs text-gray-700">
      <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-xl border border-emerald-100">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block mb-0.5">MSRP Limitations:</strong>
        Vans, SUVs, and Pickups must have an MSRP of $80,000 or less. Sedans, hatchbacks, and passenger cars must be priced at $55,000 or less.
      </div>
      <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-xl border border-emerald-100">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block mb-0.5">Income Eligibility Caps:</strong>
        Adjusted Gross Income (AGI) caps: $300,000 for married couples filing jointly; $225,000 for heads of household; $150,000 for individual single filers.
      </div>
      <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-xl border border-emerald-100">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block mb-0.5">Pre-Owned Used EV Credit:</strong>
        Eligible pre-owned EVs costing $25,000 or less purchased from a dealer qualify for up to <strong>$4,000 (30% of sales price)</strong> with lower income thresholds.
      </div>
      <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-xl border border-emerald-100">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block mb-0.5">North American Assembly:</strong>
        Final vehicle assembly must occur in North America, with critical battery mineral sourcing standards strictly verified.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 6: Maintenance & TCO -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    6. Beyond Electricity: The Hidden Maintenance Savings of EVs
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Fuel cost reduction is only half of the economic equation. An internal combustion engine relies on thousands of friction-generating moving parts that require frequent, mandatory maintenance: engine motor oil, oil filters, spark plugs, timing belts, fuel injectors, transmission fluid, catalytic converters, and oxygen sensors.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    Electric vehicle drivetrains consist of a single moving rotor inside an electric motor coupled to a single-speed reduction gearbox. There is no motor oil to change. Furthermore, because electric vehicles employ <strong>regenerative braking</strong>—using the electric motor as a generator to slow the car down while pumping energy back into the battery pack—mechanical brake pads and rotors frequently last over 100,000 to 120,000 miles before requiring replacement.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
    According to comprehensive operational telemetry analyzed in our <a href="/blog/ev-savings-2026" class="text-emerald-600 font-bold underline">5-Year Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Breakdown</a>, EV owners save an additional <strong>$500 to $800 annually in routine mechanical maintenance</strong> compared to traditional internal combustion cars.
  </p>

  <!-- Section 7: Frequently Asked Questions -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-6 tracking-tight">
    7. Frequently Asked Questions (US EV Charging)
  </h2>

  <div class="space-y-4 not-prose mb-12">
    <!-- FAQ 1 -->
    <details class="group bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden cursor-pointer shadow-xs hover:border-emerald-400 transition-all">
      <summary class="flex justify-between items-center text-sm font-black text-gray-950">
        <span>How much does it cost to charge a Tesla Model Y from 0% to 100% at home?</span>
        <span class="text-emerald-600 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i></span>
      </summary>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3 font-medium">
        A Tesla Model Y Long Range has a 75 kWh usable battery pack. Factoring in roughly 10% AC-to-DC charging loss, it draws approximately 82.5 kWh from your home utility meter. At the national average rate of $0.16 per kWh, a complete 0% to 100% recharge costs approximately <strong>$13.20</strong>, delivering around 310 miles of range (roughly 4.2 cents per mile). On off-peak night tariffs ($0.08/kWh), that cost drops to just <strong>$6.60</strong>.
      </p>
    </details>

    <!-- FAQ 2 -->
    <details class="group bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden cursor-pointer shadow-xs hover:border-emerald-400 transition-all">
      <summary class="flex justify-between items-center text-sm font-black text-gray-950">
        <span>Will charging an electric car cause my home electric bill to skyrocket?</span>
        <span class="text-emerald-600 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i></span>
      </summary>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3 font-medium">
        Your monthly electric bill will increase by roughly $45 to $65 for standard commuter mileage (1,000 miles/month). However, your gas station bill of $140 to $160 disappears entirely. In net household cash flow, you retain an extra $75 to $100+ every month. You can verify your exact local numbers using our free <a href="/#calculator-app" class="text-emerald-600 underline font-bold">VoltSavings Calculator</a>.
      </p>
    </details>

    <!-- FAQ 3 -->
    <details class="group bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden cursor-pointer shadow-xs hover:border-emerald-400 transition-all">
      <summary class="flex justify-between items-center text-sm font-black text-gray-950">
        <span>How much does a Tesla Supercharger cost compared to home charging?</span>
        <span class="text-emerald-600 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i></span>
      </summary>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3 font-medium">
        Tesla Superchargers and commercial DC fast chargers charge between $0.35 and $0.48+ per kWh, making public fast charging 2.5x to 3x more expensive than home charging. A typical 20% to 80% Supercharger session costs between $18 and $26. Supercharging is designed for intercity highway road trips; 85%+ of commuter charging should happen overnight at home for maximum financial savings.
      </p>
    </details>

    <!-- FAQ 4 -->
    <details class="group bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden cursor-pointer shadow-xs hover:border-emerald-400 transition-all">
      <summary class="flex justify-between items-center text-sm font-black text-gray-950">
        <span>How long do modern EV batteries last before needing replacement?</span>
        <span class="text-emerald-600 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i></span>
      </summary>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3 font-medium">
        Modern Lithium Iron Phosphate (LFP) batteries commonly last 2,000 to 3,000 full charge cycles, which equates to 250,000 to 400,000 miles (12 to 15+ years of daily driving). Nickel-Manganese-Cobalt (NMC) packs last 1,200 to 1,500 cycles. Federal law mandates an 8-year / 100,000-mile battery warranty on all new EVs sold in the US. Learn more in our <a href="/blog/ev-battery-life-2026" class="text-emerald-600 underline font-bold">EV Battery Lifespan & Degradation Guide</a>.
      </p>
    </details>

    <!-- FAQ 5 -->
    <details class="group bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden cursor-pointer shadow-xs hover:border-emerald-400 transition-all">
      <summary class="flex justify-between items-center text-sm font-black text-gray-950">
        <span>What is a Time-of-Use (TOU) electricity plan and how does it save EV owners money?</span>
        <span class="text-emerald-600 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down"></i></span>
      </summary>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3 font-medium">
        Most US electric utility companies offer optional Time-of-Use (TOU) plans that charge different electricity rates depending on the time of day. Peak hours (typically 4 PM to 9 PM) carry higher rates, while "super off-peak" night hours (typically 11 PM to 6 AM) drop rates to as low as $0.06 to $0.09 per kWh. By setting your EV's onboard timer to charge overnight while you sleep, you cut your charging bill in half.
      </p>
    </details>
